  "ID": "9.5",
  "Context/Background": "Intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs),  including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs), intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), and intraductal tubulopapillary neoplasms (ITPNs), are clinically detectable macroscopic non-invasive cyst-forming lesions, some of which progress to invasive pancreatic cancer. Proteins and glycoproteins have proven to be useful clinical biomarkers for classifying cyst-forming neoplasms in the pancreas.Here, we used proteomics and glycoproteomics to characterize a series of histologically and genetically well-characterized surgically resected IPNs, including IPMNs and IOPNs.\n\nOur analysis included 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs and 5 IOPNs), 76 macro-dissected grossly normal duct tissues (NDs, with 32 of the 76 NDs coming from the same resection specimens as the IPNs), and 32 cyst fluid samples (22 from matched IPMN patients, 3 matched IOPN patients, 2 from unrelated IPMN patients, and 5 other cysts). ",
  "Prompt": "We applied proteomics-based NMF subtyping strategies to the IPMNs and PDACs to explore tumor heterogeneities and reveal progression features. This analysis showed three clusters, NMF 1 was entirely composed of PDACs. NMF 2 included some PDACs and some IPMNs, while NMF 3 was almost entirely composed of IPMNs with only one PDAC. Most (75%) IPMNs with an associated PDAC were clustered in NMF 2. Use the feature genes in each of the NMF clusters and pairwise comparison between clusters to interpret the differences between NMF clusters and provide a summary of functional and clinical implications.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "openai",
  "llm_model": "o4-mini",
  "run_date": "2025-12-14 20:09:02",
  "elapsed_seconds": 347.66,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "Introduction/Context  \nWe applied non‐negative matrix factorization (NMF) to proteomic profiles of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas (PDACs) and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs). Three clusters emerged:  \n• NMF 1 (“PDAC cluster”) comprised only PDAC samples.  \n• NMF 2 (“intermediate cluster”) contained both PDACs and IPMNs (75% of IPMNs with associated PDAC fell here).  \n• NMF 3 (“IPMN cluster”) was almost wholly IPMNs (only one PDAC).  \n\nWe next extracted “feature” genes for each cluster (Table S4G) and performed pairwise differential analysis (Table S4H: NMF3 vs 1, NMF2 vs 1, NMF1 vs 2) to understand functional modules distinguishing these groups.\n\nKeywords  \n• Oxidative phosphorylation / mitochondria  \n• Extracellular matrix (ECM) organization  \n• Cytoskeleton & cell‐motility  \n• Glycoprotein secretion  \n\nCandidate Genes  \nNDUFS3, COL4A1, RAB27B, PGRMC1, ACTN4\n\n––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––  \nPathway Enrichments  \n\nBecause all pairwise DE lists were strongly skewed toward up‐regulated proteins (Up/Down = “Up” for >95% of genes in each contrast), we performed Over-Representation Analysis (ORA) on the up-regulated gene lists for each comparison.\n\n1. NMF 3 (IPMN) vs NMF 1 (PDAC) & vs NMF 2 (Intermediate)  \n   – Dominant themes: Mitochondrial energy metabolism (OXPHOS, electron transport chain), protein glycosylation and secretory machinery.  \n   – Core leading‐edge proteins: NDUFS3, NDUFA4, COX7A2L, PMM2, SEC16A.  \n\n2. NMF 2 (Intermediate) vs NMF 1 & vs NMF 3  \n   – Dominant themes: Extracellular matrix organization (collagens, basement membrane), complement and coagulation cascades, lipid transport.  \n   – Core leading‐edge proteins: COL4A1, COL6A1, APOC2, APOM, IGKC.  \n\n3. NMF 1 (PDAC) vs NMF 2 & vs NMF 3  \n   – Dominant themes: Actin‐cytoskeleton regulation, cell‐motility and invasion (lamellipodia, focal adhesion), stress response/protein folding.  \n   – Core leading‐edge proteins: ACTN4, ARPC1B/ARPC5, IFIT3, FKBP9.  \n\n––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––  \nTargeted (keyword-guided) subset  \n\nApplying our keywords to the ORA outputs:  \n• “Mitochondria”/“Oxidative phosphorylation” terms were exclusively in NMF 3 ORA (enrichment p < 1e-5).  \n• “Extracellular matrix” and “collagen” terms appeared only in NMF 2 ORA (p < 1e-6).  \n• “Actin”/“cytoskeleton” terms were unique to NMF 1 ORA (p < 1e-7).  \n\n––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––  \nKey Genes/Proteins (leading edge)  \n\nUp-regulated leading‐edge proteins only:  \n\n| Gene    | Direction | Role/Function                          | Pathway(s)                       |\n|---------|-----------|----------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| NDUFS3  | Up (NMF 3)| Complex I subunit, mitochondrial ETC   | Oxidative phosphorylation        |\n| PGRMC1  | Up (NMF 3)| Glycoprotein chaperone, steroid metabolism | Secretory/glycosylation       |\n| COL4A1  | Up (NMF 2)| Basement membrane collagen, ECM scaffold | Extracellular matrix           |\n| ACTN4   | Up (NMF 1)| Actin crosslinking, cellular motility  | Cytoskeleton regulation          |\n| ARPC1B  | Up (NMF 1)| Actin‐nucleation complex subunit       | Lamellipodia formation           |\n\n––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––  \nComparisons & Confidence  \n– NMF 3 shows a clear mitochondrial/secretory signature (high confidence: multiple ETC subunits).  \n– NMF 2 is defined by matrix remodeling and immune/lipid‐transport proteins (collagens, apolipoproteins).  \n– NMF 1 (PDAC) exhibits enriched cell‐motility machinery and stress‐response chaperones.  \n– No significant cross-cluster overlap: each cluster is driven by distinct modules.  \n\n––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––  \nImplications for Biology & Clinic  \n• Progression axis from IPMN→PDAC involves a shift from metabolic/secretory phenotype (NMF 3) to microenvironment remodeling (NMF 2) and finally to invasive motility and stress‐tolerance (NMF 1).  \n• NMF 2’s ECM signature suggests a “pre‐invasive niche” that may facilitate malignant transformation.  \n• Candidate biomarkers: PGRMC1 or NDUFS3 in cyst fluid to flag IPMN state; COL4A1/APOM for intermediate lesions; ACTN4 or IFIT3 for overt PDAC.  \n• Therapeutic targeting: mitochondrial metabolism inhibitors may stall early IPMN growth; ECM‐modulators or complement inhibitors may block transition; anti‐motility agents could curb invasion.  \n\n––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––  \nNext Steps & Clarifications  \n1. Validate PGRMC1 and NDUFS3 levels in an independent IPMN cyst‐fluid cohort.  \n2. Functionally test COL4A1 knockdown on IPMN→PDAC progression in organoid models.  \n3. Profile single‐cell proteomes across the spectrum (NMF 3→2→1) to dissect cell‐type drivers.  \n4. Check explicitly for cell‐cycle/replication signals (not observed here), ER‐stress responses (partially observed via IFIT3), and glycosylation modules (strongly observed in NMF 3).",
